<h2>Three recurring battlegrounds</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Characterisation.</strong> Parties dress unappealable orders as appealable ones — a refusal to extend time argued as an injunction matter. Appellate Divisions look at the substance of what was decided, not the label on the application.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Section 37 interface.</strong> For arbitration matters before Commercial Divisions, the appealable categories are those in Section 37 alone — orders on Section 8 references, Section 9 interim measures, Section 34 outcomes and the tribunal-stage orders listed there. Orders outside the list, including many procedural directions in arbitration petitions, do not travel up.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The Article 227 valve.</strong> Supervisory jurisdiction survives but is reserved for orders suffering jurisdictional infirmity — applying relevance where the statute demands reasonable cause, or acting without jurisdiction altogether. It is a scalpel, not a staircase.</div>
</div>

<h2>Strategic consequences</h2>
<p>The closed list changes how commercial trials are fought. First, interlocutory skirmishes lose much of their delay value: an adverse procedural order cannot detour the suit into an appellate queue, so resources are better spent on the trial record — which is also where the eventual decree appeal will be won. Second, objections must be preserved: a party aggrieved by an unappealable order should record its objection on the file, because the decree appeal is the forum where it resurfaces. Third, the sixty-day limitation under Section 13 is construed strictly in commercial matters, in keeping with the Act\'s timeline discipline — appeals should be prepared against the pronouncement date, not the certified-copy habit of ordinary civil practice. Finally, for arbitration users, the forum question (Commercial Court, Commercial Division, or the High Court\'s arbitration jurisdiction) determines the appellate court, and should be verified before filing rather than after losing.</p>

<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Before filing any appeal:</strong> locate the order in the Order XLIII or Section 37 list by provision, not by instinct.</li>
  <li><strong>Before filing an Article 227 petition:</strong> articulate the jurisdictional error in one sentence; if it cannot be done, the petition is a substitute appeal and will be treated as one.</li>
  <li><strong>Before ignoring an adverse interlocutory order:</strong> note the objection and its prejudice on record for the decree appeal.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> The Delhi High Court\'s Commercial Appellate Divisions apply these gates with increasing strictness, and recent decisions have reiterated both the narrowness of Article 227 and its availability for genuinely jurisdictional errors. The discipline cuts both ways: fewer appeals to defend, but no second chances for objections never recorded.</p>
</div>
